{"name":"Boardwalk Sashay","hex":"#c7b6aa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"boardwalk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slightly elevated path (walkway) for pedestrians over sandy or swampy ground, typically made out of wood; specifically  one running alongside a body of water or beach."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To install a boardwalk over."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boardwalk"},{"term":"sashay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chassé.","A sequence of sideways steps in a circle in square dancing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To walk casually, showily, or in a flirty manner; to strut, swagger or flounce.","To chassé when dancing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sashay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Boardwalk","blocks":[{"text":"A boardwalk is an elevated footpath, walkway, or causeway typically built with wooden planks, which functions as a type of low water bridge or small viaduct that enables pedestrians to better cross wet, muddy or marshy lands. Such timber trackways have existed since at least Neolithic times."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Atlantic_City_Boardwalk_view_north_from_Caesars_Atlantic_City_by_Silveira_Neto_June_24_2012.jpg/330px-Atlantic_City_Boardwalk_view_north_from_Caesars_Atlantic_City_by_Silveira_Neto_June_24_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boardwalk","wikidata":"Q11111030"},{"title":"Chassé","blocks":[{"text":"The chassé is a dance step used in many dances in many variations. All variations are triple-step patterns of gliding character in a \"step-together-step\" pattern. The word came from ballet terminology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Chass%C3%A9%2C_ballet_technique_tutorial.webm/330px--Chass%C3%A9%2C_ballet_technique_tutorial.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chass%C